// **`<` orders two `int`s exactly, not as `f64`.**
//
// Adjacent values near `i64::MAX` are the visible case: `f64` has 53 bits of mantissa, so both of
// these round to the same double and every comparison between them comes out `false` — including
// `a < b`, which is true. `sorted()` and `min()`/`max()` were always exact (they fold through the
// runtime's one comparator), so the operator disagreeing with them was the tell.
//
// The three doors are checked against each other here, because agreement is the property: one
// comparator serves `<`, `sorted` and the extremum reductions, and there is no second opinion for
// them to drift from.
// expect: stdout "true"
// expect: stdout "false"
// expect: stdout "false"
// expect: stdout "true"
// expect: stdout "[9223372036854775806, 9223372036854775807]"
// expect: stdout "true"
// expect: stdout "true"
// expect: stdout "true"
// expect: exit 0

a = 9223372036854775806;
b = 9223372036854775807;

echo a < b;
echo b < a;
echo a == b;
echo a <= b;

echo [b, a].sorted();
echo [b, a].min() == some(a);
echo [b, a].max() == some(b);

// And the same at the negative end, where the rounding lands the other way.
lo = -9223372036854775807;
echo lo < lo + 1;
